  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather is expected through Thursday\nwith climbing temperatures.\n\nWhat's left of the Nor'easter is over the Gulf of Maine today,\nwith cyclonic flow aloft still affecting the Mid Atlantic. A\nbroad moisture shield around the low continues to bring clouds\nacross the area, although several breaks and layers are noted on\nsatellite and in observations. Some patchy fog could develop in\nany extended breaks (as seen at St. Inigoes MD).\n\nThis moisture remains pretty consistent throughout the day, so \nmostly cloudy skies (with some breaks) will likely continue. \nWhile temperatures may be held back a few degrees, high \ntemperatures should still reach the lower to mid 70s for most \nlocations. Some models are printing spotty light QPF this \nafternoon as instability increases beneath the trough axis. \nHowever, moisture will be shallow, likely limiting any \nprecipitation to sprinkles.\n\nSkies should clear some tonight as ridging builds in at the\nsurface and aloft. This set up could be favorable for fog to\ndevelop, although the coverage remains somewhat uncertain at\nthis time. Lows will be close to the dew points in the 50s. \n\nThe surface high will move off the coast late Wednesday into\nThursday while heights continue to increase. Temperatures will \nclimb further, with more locations reaching the 80s. Some spots\ncould even approach 90 by Thursday. Developing southerly flow \nwill lead to more sunshine. Dry weather is expected both days. \nWhile nightly fog is possible, increasing southwesterly flow\njust above the surface may limit low level saturation.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Well above normal normal temperatures continue\nthrough Friday, but forecast confidence decreases moving into \nthe weekend as rain chances increase.\n\nAnother day of well above normal temperatures is expected on Friday. \n850 hPa temperatures of around 16-18C should yield high temperatures \nin the mid 80s to near 90 at lower elevations, and 70s in the \nmountains. Clouds will be on the increase through the day, and a few \nshowers can't be ruled out in western Maryland or the West Virginia \nPanhandle during the afternoon as a cold front approaches from the \nnorthwest. Guidance varies substantially from model to model and \nensemble member to ensemble member regarding the positioning of this \nfront through the upcoming weekend. With the frontal boundary in the \nvicinity, there will be at least some chance for showers either day \nthis weekend, with ensemble guidance favoring higher chances on \nSunday. Forecast uncertainty remains high however, with ensemble \nmembers showing high temperature solutions ranging from the upper \n50s to low 90s Saturday, with cooler temperatures more likely by\nSunday. The frontal zone is likely to clear the area by Monday,\nleaving below normal temperatures in its wake.",
